  "notes": [
    "The document prints no category names; categories were inferred from its colour coding (green = General Education, purple = Math & Science, red = Major Core, bold brown = Capstone, orange = Electives).",
    "CEE110 and EEE452 are coloured with the Math & Science group, so they are filed under Math & Science.",
    "Semesters follow the 12-semester prerequisite table on page 2.",
    "ENG103 prerequisite 'ENG102/Waiver' is stored as ENG102.",
    "CSE499A requires '100 credits completed'; the sequence page adds 'and all core courses', which is not encoded in the rule.",
    "'SOC101/ANT101/ENV203(GEO205)' is one slot; ENV203 and GEO205 appear to be the same course (Bangladesh Geography) under two codes, both listed as alternatives.",
    "BEN205 (alternative to ENG115) and HIS103 (alternative to HIS101) are not on the CSE PDF, which is effective Fall 2014 and predates the rule. They are added because 1.-bbt-coursemap.pdf and bs-in-mic-course-map-3.pdf state 'BEN205 and HIS103 are mandatory courses for all NSU UG students from batch 163 and onwards'. Titles are the same as in the EEE file. Credits and the total are unchanged.",
    "Courses printed with +CL (CSE115, PHY107, CSE215, PHY108, EEE141, CHE101, EEE111, BIO103; 4 credits) are split into a 3-credit theory row and a separate 1-credit lab row (code + 'L', title + ' Lab') with the same semester, category and prerequisite, because the lab is graded separately. Courses printed +IL (CSE225, CSE231, CSE311, CSE331; 3 credits) have an integrated lab inside the 3 credits and stay one row. Credits per program are unchanged (theory 3 + lab 1 = the printed 4).",
    "Elective trails: ELECT1 to ELECT3 are filled from six specialized trails, at least two courses from one trail (minFromOneTrail). The lists are the NSU CSE trail lists with titles as NSU prints them; all are 3 credits. Special topics courses (CSE491 to CSE494, CSE496) are one per trail. CSE498 Internship/Co-op is a non-credit course (minimum 8 to 12 weeks) with no prerequisite, filed under Internship in semester 12."
  ],
